ohamed Ghannouchi","from_t":2011.035616438356,"to_t":2011.0383561643835,"title":"President of Tunisia","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Mohamed Ghannouchi"},{"qid":"Q948","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q4715","value_label":"Moncef Marzouki","from_t":2011.9479452054795,"to_t":2014.9972602739726,"title":"President of Tunisia","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Moncef Marzouki"},{"qid":"Q948","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q643348","value_label":"Habib Bourguiba","from_t":1957.5616438356165,"to_t":1987.849315068493,"title":"President of Tunisia","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Habib Bourguiba"},{"qid":"Q948","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q162789","value_label":"Flag of Tunisia.svg","from_t":1831,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Tunisia","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Tunisia"}],"people":{"Q16120878":{"label":"Kais Saied","description":"Tunisian politician, academic and jurist","enwiki":"Kais Saied","summary":"Kaïs Saïed (Arabic: قَيْس سُعَيِّد, romanized: Qays Suʿayyid; born 22 February 1958) is a Tunisian politician, jurist and former assistant professor of law who has served as the fifth president of Tunisia since 2019. He was president of the Tunisian Association of Constitutional Law from 1995 to 2019.\nHaving worked in various legal and academic roles since the 1980s, Saied joined the 2019 presidential election as an independent social conservative with a populist anti-corruption platform supported by Ennahda and others across the political spectrum. He won the second round of the election with 72.71% of the vote, defeating Nabil Karoui, and was sworn in as president on 23 October 2019.","image":"Kais Saied 2023.jpg","birth":1958.1424657534246,"death":null},"Q108892374":{"label":"Sarra Zaafrani","description":"Tunisian engineer, senior civil servant and politician (born 1963)","enwiki":"Sara Zaafarani","summary":"Sara Zaafarani Zenzari (Arabic: سارة زعفراني زنزري, romanized: Sāra Zaʿfarānī Zanzarī; born 26 January 1963) is a Tunisian engineer and politician who has served as the prime minister of Tunisia since 2025. Prior to her tenure as prime minister she was minister of public works and housing.","image":"Sara Zaafarani (cropped).jpg","birth":1963.0684931506848,"death":null},"Q152369":{"label":"Fouad Mebazaa","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Fouad Mebazaa","summary":"Fouad Mebazaa (Arabic: فؤاد المبزع, romanized: Fuʾād el-Mbazaʿ; 15 June 1933 – 23 April 2025) was a Tunisian politician who was the acting president of Tunisia from 15 January 2011 to 13 December 2011. He was active in Neo Destour prior to Tunisian independence, served as Minister of Youth and Sports, Minister of Public Health, and Minister of Culture and Information, and was Speaker of the Chamber of Deputies of Tunisia from 1991 to 2011.","image":"FouedMebazaa.jpg","birth":1933.4520547945206,"death":2025.3068493150686},"Q204487":{"label":"Zine El Abidine Ben Ali","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Zine El Abidine Ben Ali","summary":"Zine El Abidine Ben Ali (Arabic: زَيْن الْعَابِدِين بْن عَلِيّ, romanized: Zayn al-ʿĀbidīn bin ʿAliyy, Tunisian Arabic: Zīn il-ʿĀbdīn bin ʿAlī; 3 September 1936 – 19 September 2019), commonly known as Ben Ali or Ezzine, was a Tunisian politician, military officer and dictator who served as the second president of Tunisia from 1987 to 2011. In that year, during the Tunisian revolution, he was overthrown and fled to Saudi Arabia.\nBen Ali was appointed Prime Minister in October 1987. He assumed the Presidency on 7 November 1987 in a bloodless coup d'état that ousted President Habib Bourguiba by declaring him incompetent.","image":"Zine El Abidine Ben Ali.jpg","birth":1936.672131147541,"death":2019.7150684931507},"Q313539":{"label":"Beji Caid Essebsi","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Beji Caid Essebsi","summary":"Beji Caid Essebsi (or es-Sebsi; Arabic: الباجي قائد السبسي, romanized: al-Bājī Qā’id as-Sibsī, ; 29 November 1926 – 25 July 2019) was a Tunisian politician who served as the fourth president of Tunisia from 2014 until his death in 2019. Previously, he served as minister of foreign affairs from 1981 to 1986 and prime minister from February to December 2011.\nEssebsi's political career spanned six decades, culminating in his leadership of Tunisia in its transition to democracy. Essebsi was the founder of the Nidaa Tounes political party, which won a plurality in the 2014 parliamentary election.","image":"Béji Caïd Essebsi 2015-05-20.jpg","birth":1926.9095890410958,"death":2019.5616438356165},"Q3318530":{"label":"Mohamed Ennaceur","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Mohamed Ennaceur","summary":"Mohamed Ennaceur (Arabic: محمد الناصر; born 21 March 1934) is a Tunisian politician who served as the acting president of Tunisia for 91 days, from President Beji Caid Essebsi's death on 25 July 2019 until he handed over the presidency to Kais Saied as the winner of the 2019 Tunisian presidential election on 23 October 2019. Since 2014, he has also been the President of the Assembly of the Representatives of the People and leader of the governing Nidaa Tounes party. Previously, he served as Minister of Social Affairs in the 1970s and 1980s under President Habib Bourguiba and again in 2011 in the transitional Ghannouchi and Essebsi governments.\nEnnaceur was the founding director of the Tunisian Association of Social Law (Revue tunisienne de droit social) and the Festival international de musique symphonique d'El Jem.","image":"Mohamed Ennaceur.jpg","birth":1934.2164383561644,"death":null},"Q431515":{"label":"Mohamed Ghannouchi","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Mohamed Ghannouchi","summary":"Mohamed Ghannouchi (Arabic: محمد الغنوشي, romanized: Muḥammad Al-Ghannūshī; born 18 August 1941) is a Tunisian politician who was Prime Minister of Tunisia from 1999 to 2011. Regarded as a technocrat, Ghannouchi was a long-standing figure in the Tunisian government under President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali. He also served as the President of Tunisia from 14 to 15 January 2011, holding the powers and duties of the office nominally for the absent President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali, who had fled the country due to the 2011 revolution. On 15 January 2011 the presidency was declared vacant by the Constitutional Court and Ben Ali's term was officially terminated, leading to Speaker of Parliament Fouad Mebazaa taking office as Acting President.","image":"Mohamed Ghannouchi.jpg","birth":1941.627397260274,"death":null},"Q4715":{"label":"Moncef Marzouki","description":"Tunisian politician","enwiki":"Moncef Marzouki","summary":"Mohamed Moncef Marzouki (Arabic: محمد المنصف المرزوقي; Muhammad al-Munṣif al-Marzūqī, born 7 July 1945) is a Tunisian politician who served as the third president of Tunisia from 2011 to 2014. Through his career he has been a human rights activist, physician and politician.  On 12 December 2011, he was elected president of Tunisia by the Constituent Assembly.","image":"Moncef Marzouki2.jpg","birth":1945.5123287671233,"death":null},"Q643348":{"label":"Habib Bourguiba","description":"Tunisian politician (1903–2000)","enwiki":"Habib Bourguiba","summary":"Habib Bourguiba (3 August 1903 – 6 April 2000) was a Tunisian politician and statesman who served as the prime minister of the Kingdom of Tunisia from 1956 to 1957, and then as the first president of Tunisia from 1957 to 1987. Prior to his presidency, he led the nation to independence from France, ending the 75-year-old protectorate and earning the title of \"Supreme Combatant\".\nBorn in Monastir to a poor family, he attended Sadiki College and Lycée Carnot in Tunis before obtaining his baccalaureate in 1924. He graduated from the University of Paris and the Paris Institute of Political Studies (Sciences Po) in 1927 and returned to Tunis to practice law.","image":"Habib Bourguiba portrait4.jpg","birth":1903.5863013698631,"death":2000.2622950819673}},"media":{"Flag of the Beylik of Tunis (1831–1881) and Tunisia (1881–1959).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_%281831%E2%80%931881%29_and_Tunisia_%281881%E2%80%931959%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_%281831%E2%80%931881%29_and_Tunisia_%281881%E2%80%931959%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_(1831%E2%80%931881)_and_Tunisia_(1881%E2%80%931959).svg","author":"This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file:","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of Arms of the beys of Tunis (Husseinic dynasty).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Coat_of_Arms_of_the_beys_of_Tunis_%28Husseinic_dynasty%29.svg/330px-Coat_of_Arms_of_the_beys_of_Tunis_%28Husseinic_dynasty%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_beys_of_Tunis_(Husseinic_dynasty).svg","author":null,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},"Flag of Tunisia.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Flag_of_Tunisia.svg/330px-Flag_of_Tunisia.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tunisia.svg","author":"Various Wikimedia Commons' contributors","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Tunisia.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Coat_of_arms_of_Tunisia.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Tunisia.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Tunisia.svg","author":"FXXX","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Kais Saied 2023.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Kais_Saied_2023.jpg/330px-Kais_Saied_2023.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_